\end_layout

\end_inset

note
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Here are two other useful ways to think about matrix-vector products.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, in terms of inner products (see 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

prf:ref
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

`def-inner-product`): the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $A\vv$
\end_inset

 consists of the inner products of each of the rows of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 with the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vv$
\end_inset

. Namely, if
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A = \begin{pmatrix}\va_1\\ \vdots\\ \va_m\end{pmatrix},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A\vv = \begin{pmatrix}\va_1\cdot\vv\\ \vdots\\ \va_m\cdot\vv\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, in terms of linear combinations: the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $A\vv$
\end_inset

 is a linear combination of the columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 where the coefficients are the entries of 
\begin_inset Formula $\vv$
\end_inset

. Namely, if
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A = (\va_1\ \va_2\ \cdots\ \va_n)\quad\text{and}\quad
\vv = \begin{pmatrix}v_1\\ \vdots\\ v_n\end{pmatrix},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
A\vv = v_1 \va_1 + v_2 \va_2 + \cdots + v_n \va_m.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
## Matrix multiplication
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 can be multiplied if the number of columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 equals the number of rows of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

prf:definition
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 If 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times n$
\end_inset

-matrix and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times p$
\end_inset

-matrix, then the *(matrix) product* of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times p$
\end_inset

-matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $AB$
\end_inset

 defined as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
(AB)_{i,k} = \sum_{j=1}^n A_{i,j} B_{j,k}
\quad(1\le i\le m,1\le k\le p).
\]
\end_inset

:::
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The product 
\begin_inset Formula $AB$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 matrix-vector multiplications: if 
\begin_inset Formula $B=(\vb_1\ \vb_2\ \cdots \vb_p)$
\end_inset

, then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset Formula \[
AB = (A\vb_1\ A\vb_2\ \cdots A\vb_p).
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
:::
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\end_layout

\end_inset

prf:property
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Properties of matrix multiplication For matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 and scalars 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 the following hold whenever the expressions are defined:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- 
\begin_inset Formula $A(B + C)= AB + AC$
\end_inset

 - 
\begin_inset Formula $(A + B)C = AC + BC$
\end_inset

 - 
\begin_inset Formula $(AB)C = A(BC)$
\end_inset

 - 
\begin_inset Formula $(cA)B = c(AB) = A(cB)$
\end_inset

 :::
\end_layout

